person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Westphal</surname><given-names>R.S.</given-names></name><name><surname>Tavalin</surname><given-names>S.J.</given-names></name><name><surname>Lin</surname><given-names>J.W.</given-names></name><name><surname>Alto</surname><given-names>N.M.</given-names></name><name><surname>Fraser</surname><given-names>I.D.</given-names></name><name><surname>Langeberg</surname><given-names>L.K.</given-names></name><name><surname>Sheng</surname><given-names>M.</given-names></name><name><surname>Scott</surname><given-names>J.D.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Regulation of NMDA receptors by an associated phosphatase-kinase signaling complex</article-title><source>Science.</source><volume>285</volume><year>1999</year><fpage>93</fpage><lpage>96</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10390370</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="Wilson2000"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Wilson</surname><given-names>K.L.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>The nuclear envelope, muscular dystrophy and gene expression</article-title><source>Trends Cell Biol.</source><volume>10</volume><year>2000</year><fpage>125</fpage><lpage>129</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10740265</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="Witczaketal1999"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Witczak</surname><given-names>O.</given-names></name><name><surname>Sk&#x000e5;lhegg</surname><given-names>B.S.</given-names></name><name><surname>Keryer</surname><given-names>G.</given-names></name><name><surname>Bornens</surname><given-names>M.</given-names></name><name><surname>Task&#x000e9;n</surname><given-names>K.</given-names></name><name><surname>Jahnsen</surname><given-names>T.</given-names></name><name><surname>&#x000d8;rstavik</surname><given-names>S.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Cloning and characterization of a cDNA encoding an A-kinase anchoring protein located in the centrosome, AKAP450</article-title><source>EMBO (Eur. Mol. Biol. Organ.) J.</source><volume>18</volume><year>1999</year><fpage>1858</fpage><lpage>1868</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="WormanandCourvalin2000"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Worman</surname><given-names>H.J.</given-names></name><name><surname>Courvalin</surname><given-names>J.-C.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>The inner nuclear membrane</article-title><source>J. Membr. Biol.</source><volume>In press</volume><year>2000</year></citation></ref><ref id="Wormanetal1990"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Worman</surname><given-names>H.J.</given-names></name><name><surname>Evans</surname><given-names>C.</given-names></name><name><surname>Blobel</surname><given-names>G.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>The lamin B receptor of the nuclear envelope inner membranea polytopic protein with eight potential transmembrane domains</article-title><source>J. Cell Biol.</source><volume>111</volume><year>1990</year><fpage>1535</fpage><lpage>1542</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">2170422</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="Zhangetal1996"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Zhang</surname><given-names>Q.</given-names></name><name><surname>Carr</surname><given-names>D.W.</given-names></name><name><surname>Lerea</surname><given-names>K.M.</given-names></name><name><surname>Scott</surname><given-names>J.D.</given-names></name><name><surname>Newman</surname><given-names>S.A.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Nuclear localization of type II cAMP-dependent protein kinase during limb cartilage differentiation is associated with a novel developmentally regulated A-kinase anchoring protein</article-title><source>Dev. Biol.</source><volume>176</volume><year>1996</year><fpage>51</fpage><lpage>61</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8654894</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="ZhaoandLee1997"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Zhao</surname><given-names>S.</given-names></name><name><surname>Lee</surname><given-names>E.Y.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>A protein phosphatase-1-binding motif identified by the panning of a random peptide display library</article-title><source>J. Biol. Chem.</source><volume>272</volume><year>1997</year><fpage>28368</fpage><lpage>28372</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9353294</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="ZolnierowiczandBollen2000"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Zolnierowicz</surname><given-names>S.</given-names></name><name><surname>Bollen</surname><given-names>M.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Protein phosphorylation and protein phosphatases</article-title><source>EMBO (Eur. Mol. Biol. Organ.) J.</source><volume>19</volume><year>2000</year><fpage>483</fpage><lpage>488</lpage></citation></ref></ref-list><fn-group><fn><p><italic>Abbreviations used in this paper:</italic> AKAP, A-kinase anchoring protein&#x0003b; LBR, lamin B receptor&#x0003b; NE, nuclear envelope&#x0003b; PKA, protein kinase A&#x0003b; PP1, protein phosphatase type 1.</p></fn></fn-group></back><floats-wrap><fig position="float" id="F1"><label>Figure 1</label><caption><p>AKAP149 is a protein of the ER/NE network. (A) RII overlay of HeLa cell NE proteins using <sup>32</sup>P-labeled RII&#x003b1; as a probe without or with 100 nM Ht31 competitor peptide (left). Asterisks indicate RII-binding proteins&#x0003b; arrowhead points to RII. The filters were also immunoblotted using anti-AKAP95 and anti-AKAP149 mAbs (right). (B) Structural domains of AKAP149. TM, predicted transmembrane domain. KGVLF represents a putative PP1-binding domain. AKAP49 also contains PKA-RII&#x02013;binding and putative RNA-binding (KH) domains. aa, amino acids. (C) Distribution of AKAP149 in unsynchronized HeLa cells was examined by immunofluorescence using the anti-AKAP149 mAb. (D) Interphase and mitotic HeLa cells were fractionated into nuclei or chromatin, cytosol, and cytoplasmic membranes and each fraction was immunoblotted using anti-AKAP149 mAbs. (E) Interphase membrane vesicles were fractionated by floatation in a sucrose density gradient, and fractions were immunoblotted using anticalnexin and anti-AKAP149 antibodies. P, residual pellet. Bar, 10 &#x003bc;m.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JCB0006090.f1"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F2"><label>Figure 2</label><caption><p>AKAP149 is an integral membrane protein of the NE. (A) Purified HeLa nuclei were fractionated into chromatin, matrices, and NEs and distribution of AKAP149 in each fraction was determined by immunoblotting. (B) Immunofluorescence colocalization of AKAP149 with either LBR (top) or lamin B (bottom) in isolated HeLa nuclei. DNA was labeled with Hoechst 33342. (C) Purified HeLa NEs were extracted with 1% Triton X-100, 1 M NaCl, 1% Triton X-100/1 M NaCl, or 1 N NaOH. Unextracted (P) and extracted proteins (S) were immunoblotted using anti-AKAP149 mAb. (D) Purified HeLa cell microsomes (Input mic.) were exposed to either no trypsin, 100 &#x003bc;g/ml trypsin, or 100 &#x003bc;g/ml trypsin plus 100 &#x003bc;g/ml soybean trypsin inhibitor (SBTI), for 15 min and solubilized in SDS sample buffer. Accessibility of AKAP149 and of the ER protein calnexin to trypsin was analyzed by immunoblotting. Bars, 10 &#x003bc;m.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JCB0006090.f2"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F3"><label>Figure 3</label><caption><p>AKAP149 binds PP1 in vitro and in vivo. (A) Binding of PP1 to AKAP149. AKAP149 and control immune precipitates (IP) immobilized on nitrocellulose (AKAP149 blot) were incubated with either recombinant PP1 alone, PP1 plus 10 &#x003bc;M competitor peptide PP1-BD, PP1 plus 10 &#x003bc;M control PP1-BD(V155A) peptide, or PP1 plus 10 &#x003bc;M RII-anchoring inhibitor peptide Ht31. PP1 binding was detected by immunoblotting using the anti-PP1 mAb. (B) Affinity purification of PP1 with microcystin-sepharose beads from interphase cell lysates. Immunoblot analysis was carried out on input lysate (Input), microcystin-treated lysate (Unbound), and eluates from microcystin-sepharose beads (Bound) using anti-PP1 and anti-AKAP149 mAbs. Control isolations were performed with protein A&#x02013;Sepharose beads. (C) Left panel, immunoblot of nuclei and NEs with anti-PP1 mAb. Right panels, AKAP149 and PP1 were immunoprecipitated (IP) from NE extracts and precipitates were immunoblotted using anti-PP1 and anti-AKAP149 mAbs. IgG, control immunoprecipitation with nonimmune mouse IgGs.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JCB0006090.f3"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F4"><label>Figure 4</label><caption><p>Recruitment of nuclear PP1 to the NE upon nuclear reassembly in vitro. Condensed chromatin substrates were incubated in an interphase extract containing membrane vesicles. (A) Nuclear reassembly was monitored by phase&#x02013;contrast microscopy (top). DNA was stained with Hoechst 33342. Assembly of nuclear membranes and PP1 distribution were examined by immunofluorescence analysis of methanol-fixed nuclei, using anti-LBR rabbit polyclonal antibodies and anti-PP1 mAbs, respectively. Nuclear lamina assembly was visualized using anti-lamin B antibodies. (B) PP1 distribution and targeting of AKAP149 to the reforming nucleus were monitored by double immunofluorescence using anti-PP1 polyclonal antibodies and the anti-AKAP149 mAb. Note the association of PP1 with the nuclear periphery only when AKAP149 is assembled into the NE (&#x0223c;60 min). Bars, 10 &#x003bc;m.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JCB0006090.f4"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F5"><label>Figure 5</label><caption><p>AKAP149 anchors nuclear PP1 at the NE. (A) Inhibition of PP1 recruitment to the NE by PP1-BD. Nuclei were reassembled in vitro as in the legend to <xref ref-type="fig" rid="F4">Fig. 4</xref>&#x0003b; however, the interphase cytosol was preincubated with either no peptide, 10 &#x003bc;M of the PP1-binding competitor peptide PP1-BD, or 10 &#x003bc;M of control peptide PP1-BD(V155A). An ATP-generating system was added to promote nuclear reassembly at 30&#x000b0;C. After 2 h, the NE distribution of AKAP149 and PP1 was examined by phase&#x02013;contrast and by immunofluorescence analysis using the anti-AKAP149 mAb and anti-PP1 polyclonal antibodies. (B) Immunodepletion of AKAP149 from mitotic membrane vesicles. Mitotic vesicles were solubilized with 0.5% NP-40/0.4 M KCl, and AKAP149 was immunodepleted by two successive rounds of immunoprecipitation. Mock depletions were performed with nonimmune mouse IgGs. AKAP149-depleted and mock-depleted vesicles were reconstituted by dialysis, washed, and aliquots were dissolved in SDS sample buffer. AKAP149 immunodepletion was monitored by immunoblotting each fraction. (C) PP1 is not recruited to AKAP149-depleted NEs. Nuclei were reassembled in interphase cytosol using membrane vesicles either mock-depleted (left) or immunodepleted of AKAP149 (right). Distribution of AKAP149 and PP1 was determined by immunofluorescence. Bars, 10 &#x003bc;m.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JCB0006090.f5"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F6"><label>Figure 6</label><caption><p>Restitution of AKAP149 in nuclear membranes restores recruitment of exogenous PP1 to the NE. Nuclei reconstituted with AKAP149-depleted vesicles as in <xref ref-type="fig" rid="F5">Fig. 5</xref> C were sedimented through sucrose and resuspended into fresh interphase cytosol containing combinations of recombinant PP1, AKAP149-depleted vesicles (control), mock-depleted vesicles (harboring AKAP149), and 10 &#x003bc;M of the PP1-binding competitor peptide PP1-BD. GTP was added to all reactions to promote fusion of vesicles with nuclear membranes. After 1.5 h, distribution of AKAP149 and PP1 was examined by immunofluorescence. Bar, 10 &#x003bc;m.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JCB0006090.f6"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F7"><label>Figure 7</label><caption><p>Targeting of PP1 to the NE by AKAP149 correlates with lamin B assembly. (A) PP1-BD prevents lamin B assembly. Nuclei were reassembled in the absence of competitor peptide, or with 10 &#x003bc;M PP1-BD or control PP1-BD(V155A), as in <xref ref-type="fig" rid="F5">Fig. 5</xref> A. Targeting of PP1 to the NE and assembly of lamin B were analyzed by immunofluorescence. (B) AKAP149-depleted nuclei do not assemble lamin B. Nuclei were reassembled from mock-depleted or AKAP149-depleted vesicles as in <xref ref-type="fig" rid="F5">Fig. 5</xref> B, and targeting of PP1 and lamin B to the NE was monitored by immunofluorescence as in A. (C) Restitution of AKAP149 in the NE restores lamin B assembly. Nuclei reformed from AKAP149-depleted vesicles as in B were exposed to fresh interphase cytosol containing combinations of PP1, AKAP149-depleted vesicles, mock-depleted vesicles, and PP1-BD as in the legend to <xref ref-type="fig" rid="F6">Fig. 6</xref>. After 1.5 h, PP1 targeting to the NE and lamin B assembly were immunofluorescently examined. Insets, DNA staining with Hoechst 33342. Bars, 10 &#x003bc;m.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JCB0006090.f7"/></fig></floats-wrap></article>